rk915: poll the rx path while waiting for RESET_COMPLETE

The "No reset complete after N ticks" boot failure is intermittent, and
wait_for_reset_complete() was a bare wait_event_timeout(): the completion
only arrives via the rx thread, which only runs when the host-wake line
fires, so one missed edge right after the download costs the whole
timeout. Kick the rx thread every 100ms while waiting so it polls the
chip for the pending event. A firmware that never answers still times
out identically.

The same structure, with the same reasoning, is in the independent
sv6160 driver ("Poll the RX worker manually since SDIO interrupts may
not be firing during early init").

--- a/src/utils.c
+++ b/src/utils.c
@@ -131,7 +131,32 @@
 	else
 		timeout = msecs_to_jiffies(3000);
 
-	wait_event_timeout(priv->hal->wait_q, priv->reset_complete, timeout);
+	if (enable) {
+		/*
+		 * RESET_COMPLETE only reaches us through the rx thread, and
+		 * the rx thread only runs when the host-wake line fires. A
+		 * single missed edge during early init - the line is
+		 * level-triggered and the irq has just been re-enabled after
+		 * the download - otherwise costs the whole timeout and shows
+		 * up as "No reset complete after N ticks" on some boots.
+		 *
+		 * So do not just sleep on it: kick the rx thread every 100ms
+		 * so it goes and looks. A firmware that really never answers
+		 * still times out here exactly as before.
+		 */
+		unsigned long deadline = jiffies + timeout;
+		unsigned long slice = msecs_to_jiffies(100);
+
+		while (!priv->reset_complete &&
+		       time_before(jiffies, deadline)) {
+			hal_irq_handler(priv->hal);
+			wait_event_timeout(priv->hal->wait_q,
+					   priv->reset_complete, slice);
+		}
+	} else {
+		wait_event_timeout(priv->hal->wait_q, priv->reset_complete,
+				   timeout);
+	}
 
 	if (!priv->reset_complete) {
 		rk915_err("%s-UMAC: No reset complete after %d ticks\n",
